{"assets":["/editorial-assets/0923fabb9b54bf22a6cb7614f98c9a606dbeca94c8193acb5b80afc0910054ed.png","/editorial-assets/362b7b19b2c30e4bfd0aaa9f9f1dce2cec63a04dd5f6f9ddc5f487cb119ecc39.png","/editorial-assets/c0ed846a934088c5b50fba341c1b4aef0e9b04104aa5f4ddfcc837e1f9ef746e.png","/editorial-assets/bc6d8fccf4fb1c11410f30edc23b6c2e2e0247b605d06459373e846c333967fc.png","/editorial-assets/7f9f366e0728e2b25a15334d3d6adff9e79eb0f9c379d58955f8f8c233d9a353.png","/editorial-assets/7580ca2cf4541758403ab75ce99786658a2f80805e7a3c10ee51be4d308f4acc.png","/editorial-assets/1d1e18ae51ad514eb301ed71b0246cc980976dfc1d06b71f462e2736340345ea.png","/editorial-assets/04233d2578bf0657f498a1c61aa78fd84cedbb5bec10613fa32e6ac27305e607.png"],"contestId":"277","diagnostics":[],"document":{"children":[{"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/278/problem/B"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"278B - New Problem"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The total number of different strings of 2 letters is 26"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 676, but the total length of the input strings is no more than 600. It means that the length of answer is no more than 2. So just check all the strings of length 1 and 2."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/277/problem/A"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"277A - Learning Languages"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Build bipartite graph with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" nodes for employees and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" nodes for languages. If an employee initially knows a language, than there will be an edge between corresponding nodes. Now the problem is simple: add the minimal number of edges in such a way, that all the "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" employees will be in the same connected component. Obviously, this number equals to the number of initially connected components, containing at least one employee, minus one. But there is one exception (pretest #4): if initially everyone knows no languages, we'll have to add "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" edges, because we can't add the edges between employees (remember that the graph is bipartite)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/277/problem/B"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"277B - Set of Points"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"For "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 3, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 5 and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 3, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 6 there is no solution."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's learn how to construct the solution for "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≥ 5 and is odd. Set up "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" points on a circle of sufficiently large radius. This will be the inner polygon. The outer polygon will be the inner polygon multiplied by 2. More precisely (1 ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"):"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/0923fabb9b54bf22a6cb7614f98c9a606dbeca94c8193acb5b80afc0910054ed.png"},"kind":"image"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/362b7b19b2c30e4bfd0aaa9f9f1dce2cec63a04dd5f6f9ddc5f487cb119ecc39.png"},"kind":"image"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/c0ed846a934088c5b50fba341c1b4aef0e9b04104aa5f4ddfcc837e1f9ef746e.png"},"kind":"image"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/bc6d8fccf4fb1c11410f30edc23b6c2e2e0247b605d06459373e846c333967fc.png"},"kind":"image"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"If "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is even, construct the solution for "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1 and then delete one point from each polygon. If "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" < 2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", delete 2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" points from the inner polygon."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Unfortunately, this solution doesn't work for "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 4, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 7 and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 4, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 8."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Another approach is to set up "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" points on a convex function (for example, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 10"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"7"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"), and set up the rest "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" points on a concave function (for example, "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"y"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" =  - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 10"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"7"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"). Take a look at "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/rng_58"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"rng_58"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":"'s solution — "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/277/submission/3210150"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"3210150"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/277/problem/C"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"277C - Game"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"At first, notice that horizontal and vertical cuts are independent. Consider a single horizontal line. It contains "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" unit segments. And in any game state it's always possible to decrease the number of uncut units as the player wants. Imagine, that she starts growing a segment from a border, increasing it's length by 1 at a time. Each time the total uncut length decreases by either 0 or 1. In the end it obviously reaches 0."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The same holds for vertical lines as well. So if there are no initial cuts, the game is a nim with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1 piles of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" stones and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1 piles of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" stones. Could be solved with simple formula."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Initial "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" cuts should be just a technical difficulty. For any vertical/horizontal line, which contains at least one of the cuts, it's pile size should be decreased by the total length of all segments on this line."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"How to make a first move in nim: let "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"res"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is the result of state (grundy function), and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" is the size of the "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"-th pile. Then the result of the game without "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"-th pile is "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/7f9f366e0728e2b25a15334d3d6adff9e79eb0f9c379d58955f8f8c233d9a353.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":". We want to replace "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" with some "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", so that "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/7580ca2cf4541758403ab75ce99786658a2f80805e7a3c10ee51be4d308f4acc.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":". Obviously, the only possible "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/1d1e18ae51ad514eb301ed71b0246cc980976dfc1d06b71f462e2736340345ea.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":". The resulting solution: find a pile for which "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/04233d2578bf0657f498a1c61aa78fd84cedbb5bec10613fa32e6ac27305e607.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":", and decrease it downto "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/7f9f366e0728e2b25a15334d3d6adff9e79eb0f9c379d58955f8f8c233d9a353.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/277/problem/D"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"277D - Google Code Jam"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Suppose we have fixed set of inputs that we have to solve. Let's learn how to determine the optimal order. Obviously, Small inputs (and Large inputs with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"probFail"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 0) won't fail in any case. It means that our penalty time is no less than submission time of last such ``safe'' inputs. So we will solve such inputs before all the others. Inputs with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"probFail"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" = 1 are just a waste of time, we won't solve such inputs. Now we have only inputs with 0 < "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"probFail"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" < 1. Let "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" be two problems that we are going to solve consecutively at some moment. Let's check, if it is optimal to solve them in order "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", or in reversed order. We can discard all the other inputs, because they don't affect on the relative order of these two."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"timeLarge"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"timeLarge"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":")(1 - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"probFail"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":") + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"timeLarge"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"(1 - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"probFail"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":")"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"probFail"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" < ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"timeLarge"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"timeLarge"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":")(1 - "},{"childre